from pathlib import Path
import json
from reportlab.platypus import SimpleDocTemplate,Paragraph,PageBreak,Table,TableStyle,Spacer
from reportlab.lib.styles import getSampleStyleSheet,ParagraphStyle
from reportlab.lib import colors
from reportlab.pdfbase import pdfmetrics
from reportlab.pdfbase.ttfonts import TTFont
from matplotlib.font_manager import findfont,FontProperties
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;DEST=R.parent/'final_audited'
S=[
('Economic narratives and consumer sentiment',35,'My project asks whether economic news helps explain weak US consumer sentiment beyond economic conditions. I compare three explanations: household pressures, media information and framing, and measurement or model limitations. I collected and coded original public titles, then connected their monthly tone to linear regressions. The main result is conditional. News helps a narrow economic model in the observed sample, but that benefit does not survive broader controls and recent sentiment. I therefore do not establish media bias as the main cause of pessimism.'),
('The economic baseline and its gap',45,'The outcome is the Michigan sentiment index, which combines views about finances, business conditions, and buying conditions. It is not a percentage of optimistic people. The economic baseline uses unemployment, annual PCE inflation, and quarterly annualized GDP growth. A separate long-history evaluation covers 81 months. Its average error since 2022 is minus 14.85 index points, meaning observed sentiment is below the model prediction. That gap is model-dependent. Missing household pressures, expectations, politics, and survey differences can all contribute. Lower inflation also does not reverse the increase in prices that households already face.'),
('Our original NLP measurement',50,'The original sample contains 670 relevant titles from NPR, Colorado Sun, and Hacker News across 47 balanced months. Each source gets equal weight. A title receives minus one for adverse conditions, zero for mixed or undirected language, and plus one for favorable conditions. We average within source and then across sources. This measures content supply, not what survey respondents read. Automatic classifiers transferred poorly, so the primary measure uses frozen assistant labels. A repeated AI review agreed on 147 of 150 items. That is internal consistency. The required two independent human ratings are still pending and are a material limitation.'),
('Three models and a matched control',55,'M1 contains unemployment, inflation, and GDP. M2 adds our original tone measure. M3 adds cumulative prices, real-income growth, a presidency-era indicator, and a survey-era indicator. We also remove tone from M3 to isolate its contribution. Each model trains initially on 24 monthly observations and then predicts the same 21 later targets. M1 has RMSE 12.07. M2 improves to 10.82. M3 gives 6.06, but its identical controls without tone already give 5.82. The M1-to-M3 improvement therefore cannot be credited to media. The narrow news gain has an uncertainty interval that crosses zero.'),
('Recent sentiment is a demanding benchmark',45,'RMSE measures typical prediction error in index points, with extra weight on large mistakes. Lower is better. Sentiment persists, so a serious forecasting model must compete with recent sentiment. Simply predicting last month unchanged gives 4.09 points of error on these same targets. Dynamic controls give 3.98. Adding our tone measure raises that error to 4.32. These point estimates challenge the claim that the original AI index offers reliable additional information. They do not prove that news never matters, and the small, previously inspected test sample does not establish a permanent winning model.'),
('The media association weakens with controls',50,'The narrow regression associates a 0.1 increase in tone with 1.99 additional sentiment points. With full controls, that becomes 0.52, and its interval crosses zero. With prior sentiment, it falls to 0.17 and remains uncertain. These are conditional associations, not the effect of changing media exposure. Some predictors strongly overlap, and the controlled model uses nine parameters with only 24 initial observations. The inputs also use revised historical snapshots and assumed posting buffers. We therefore describe the forecasts as retrospective. We do not claim that every input matches the exact value available in history.'),
('Framing requires a factual benchmark',45,'A concrete example comes from the July 2020 GDP release. One title reports a 32.9 percent decline, while another reports 9.5 percent. The first is an annualized rate. The second is the actual quarterly decline. Both magnitudes can match the same event, but omitting the unit creates ambiguity. We flag that title-level framing issue without declaring an unseen whole article false. Other selected titles accurately describe favorable jobs and inflation news. Negative wording, emotional language, numerical accuracy, and reader bias are separate concepts. These purposive cases do not estimate the share of biased coverage.'),
('A causal test and prospective monitoring',50,'The present analysis cannot identify national media causality because it lacks respondent exposure. The permitted extension is a detailed causal-design proposal. After institutional review, the proposal randomizes 600 adults among three equally factual summaries: price-level emphasis, inflation-rate emphasis, and balanced emphasis. The facts and source stay constant. We measure changes in beliefs, recall, and sentiment-related responses. No experiment has run. Even a causal vignette effect would require representative exposure and transport assumptions to explain the national Michigan index. The dashboard supports future monitoring, with missingness, intervals, and preliminary observations visible.'),
('What the evidence supports',45,'My conclusion is that the narrow economic baseline misses important information, while our AI tone index does not reliably improve the controlled comparisons. I cannot assign a causal share of pessimism to media, household strain, politics, or survey changes. One dictionary sensitivity improves a dynamic forecast, so there is conflicting exploratory evidence that deserves future validation. For financial forecasting, the practical lesson is to compare text signals with persistence and household information before using them. Independent human validation and historical-vintage reconstruction remain unfinished. Those limits are part of the result, not hidden behind improved regression fit.')]
(R/'outputs/speaking_script.json').write_text(json.dumps(S,indent=2))
for n,w in [('D','normal'),('DB','bold')]:pdfmetrics.registerFont(TTFont(n,findfont(FontProperties(family='DejaVu Sans',weight=w))))
st=getSampleStyleSheet()
for n,size,lead,f,col in [('B',10.5,15,'D','#263B4C'),('H',21,27,'DB','#17293D'),('S',12,17,'DB','#008577')]:st.add(ParagraphStyle(name=n,fontName=f,fontSize=size,leading=lead,spaceAfter=9,textColor=colors.HexColor(col)))
def P(t,k='B'):return Paragraph(t,st[k])
def footer(c,d):c.setFont('D',8);c.drawString(48,26,'Khushi Khanna / Presentation and understanding guide');c.drawRightString(547,26,str(d.page))
parts=[]
def page(title,text):parts.extend([P(title,'H')]+text+[PageBreak()])
page('Understand the argument before rehearsing',[
P('The one-sentence answer','S'),P('Our original news-tone measure adds little dependable information after broader controls and previous sentiment, so the evidence does not establish media bias as the cause of pessimism.'),
P('The complete chain','S'),P('Public titles become labels. Labels become one balanced monthly index. Earlier index values enter regressions. Predictions are compared with actual sentiment on the same dates. The text data therefore feed the main statistical analysis.'),
P('Keep the units and samples separate','S'),P('670 titles produce 47 monthly index readings. Timing gives 45 finalized regression observations. The first 24 train the initial models. The next 21 evaluate predictions. The economic baseline uses a separate 81-month test. Never say that the regression has 670 independent monthly observations.'),
P('The calculation in plain language','S'),P('For a source with five labels of -1, -1, 0, 0, and +1, the mean is -0.2. Average that source mean equally with NPR and the other source. A 0.1 movement is a tenth of this -1 to +1 scale. It is not a percentage of Americans.'),
P('What is unfinished?','S'),P('The assignment requires two independent humans to label 300 items. The supplied forms are blank. The historical evaluation uses revised snapshots and assumed posting lags. Neither a high AI agreement score nor a numerical audit removes those limitations.')])
for start,end in [(0,3),(3,6),(6,9)]:
 page('Your 7-minute script: slides '+str(start+1)+'-'+str(end),[item for title,secs,text in S[start:end] for item in [P(title+' / '+str(secs)+' seconds','S'),P(text)]])
qa=[
('What exactly are the three required models?','M1 uses three economic indicators. M2 adds our original AI tone. M3 adds direct household and era controls. The controls-only comparison tests the contribution of tone separately.'),
('Why not call the 6.06 error a media success?','Controls without tone already achieve 5.82. Adding tone worsens that matched comparison. Comparing M1 with M3 changes too many variables to identify the media contribution.'),
('Does 98% agreement mean 98% accurate?','No. It means two same-session AI passes agree on 147 of 150 titles. They can share mistakes. Independent human accuracy is not established.'),
('Why is the gap not bias?','It is actual sentiment minus a prediction from a specific model. The model can omit real pressures, expectations, political differences, and measurement changes.'),
('Can negative news still be correct?','Yes. A report of job losses can accurately describe an adverse fact. Bias requires an appropriate benchmark for selection, context, or accuracy.'),
('What is R-squared versus RMSE?','R-squared describes fit to observed variation. Adjusted R-squared penalizes added predictors. Forecast RMSE measures mistakes on later observations. High fit can coexist with poor predictions.'),
('Why does the dictionary look better?','It improves one secondary dynamic point estimate. We retain that evidence, but do not select it after seeing outcomes or claim its measurement is validated.'),
('What does the presidency variable mean?','It is a calendar-era indicator. It does not measure anyone\'s party or isolate the effect of a president. Many policies and shocks change at the same time.'),
('What would make the project stronger?','Complete independent human labeling. Reconstruct release vintages. Improve balanced historical coverage. Freeze the next forecast before observing outcomes. Collect exposure or experimental evidence for causal claims.'),
('Can I say the assignment is fully complete?','No. The report and package explicitly disclose the missing independent human validation and incomplete vintage timing. Use the supplied forms to complete the human requirement, then rerun its evaluator and review the conclusions.')]
page('Questions to practise',[item for q,a in qa[:5] for item in [P(q,'S'),P(a)]])
page('Questions to practise, continued',[item for q,a in qa[5:] for item in [P(q,'S'),P(a)]])
parts.pop();SimpleDocTemplate(str(DEST/'Presentation_Speaking_Notes.pdf'),pagesize=(595.28,841.89),leftMargin=48,rightMargin=48,topMargin=43,bottomMargin=45).build(parts,onFirstPage=footer,onLaterPages=footer)
print('Script seconds:',sum(s[1] for s in S),'words:',sum(len(s[2].split()) for s in S))
